// Three vertical stripes, blue at the pole, equal on land, on a 2:3 grid.
vexyl 1
meta {
  name "France"
  encoder "Naomi Persephone Amethyst"
  license "CC-BY-SA-4.0"
  render-license "CC0-1.0"
  source "https://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/le-bloc-de-constitutionnalite/texte-integral-de-la-constitution-du-4-octobre-1958-en-vigueur"
  source "https://www.elysee.fr/la-presidence/le-drapeau-francais"
  source "https://www.crwflags.com/fotw/flags/fr.html"
  source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_of_France"
  note "Article 2 of the constitution gives only the tricolor, blue, white, red. The order from the pole is the Convention's decree of 27 pluviôse an II, as the Élysée quotes it. The decree itself wasn't read."
  note "The 2:3 proportion and equal stripes aren't in any law read here. They're custom, as given by the Album des Pavillons 2000 through FOTW, and by Wikipedia."
  note "FOTW reports stripes of 30:33:37 for use at sea, from a regulation of 17 May 1853. That wasn't read, so it isn't included as a variant."
  note "No text fixes the shades. The hex, Pantone and CMYK values are the Album des Pavillons 2023's, as reported by FOTW. A lighter blue, #0055a4, was also in use from the 1970s, and reports say the Élysée returned to the dark blue in July 2020 with no text or order. White is plain white."
  law "Constitution of the French Republic, 4 October 1958, Article 2" {
    source "https://www.conseil-constitutionnel.fr/le-bloc-de-constitutionnalite/texte-integral-de-la-constitution-du-4-octobre-1958-en-vigueur"
  }
  law "Decree of the National Convention of 27 pluviôse an II (15 February 1794)"
}
palette {
  blue "#002654" {
    pantone "2738 C"
    cmyk 100 92 21 6
  }
  white "#ffffff"
  red "#e1000f" {
    pantone "2347 C"
    cmyk 0 100 100 0
  }
}
proportion hoist=2 fly=3

bars blue white red {
  along hoist
}
